                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The concept of
            %em Fréchet manifold
            is a special case of that of
            = succeed ":" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinite-dimensional+manifold"} infinite-dimensional manifold
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            modeled on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+space"} Cartesian space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
            %em Fréchet manifold
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            modeled on a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+space"} Fréchet space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn ∞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
            (
            %a{:href => "Fr&#233;chet+space#ProjRInfinity"}> exmpl.
            ).
          %p
            The
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            of Fréchet manifolds is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FFEmbeddingOfFrechetInDiffeological"}
            below) hence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "diffeological+space#EmbeddingOfDiffeologicalSpacesIntoTheSheafTopos"} here
          %h2#definition Definition
          %h3#frchet_manifolds Fréchet manifolds
          %p
            It is possible to define, analogous to the finite dimensional case, the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+spaces"} Fréchet spaces
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "Fr&#233;chet+space#DifferentiableAndSmoothFunctions"} Fréchet space – Differentiable and smooth functions
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifold"} smooth manifold
            carries over word by word:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong Fréchet manifold
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+space"} Hausdorff topological space
              with an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/atlas"} atlas
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coordinate+charts"} coordinate charts
              taking their value in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+spaces"} Fréchet spaces
          %p It is possible to generalize some concepts of differential geometry from the finite case to the Fréchet case, one has to be careful, however:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dual+vector+space"} dual
                of a Fréchet space that is not a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+space"} Banach space
                is never a Fréchet space, therefore one cannot e.g. define both the tangent and the cotangent bundle as Fréchet manifolds. More serious is however
            %li
              %p The existence and uniqueness theorems for ordinary differential equations fail in infinite dimensions, so that theorems depending on that from finite dimensional differential geometry cannot be transcribed to the infinite situation in general. It is possible to do this on a case by case basis however.
          %h3#tangent_vectors Tangent Vectors
          %p There are several definitions of tangent vectors that are equivalent in the finite dimensional setting, but may be different in infinite dimensions. Tangent vectors can be defined to be derivations on germs of functions (algebraic definition), or as equivalence classes of smooth curves (kinematic definition). For the time being we settle with the kinematic definition:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              %strong kinematic tangent vector
            %p
              The kinematic tangent vector space of a Fréchet manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              at a point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              consists of all pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p, c'(0))
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
              is a smooth curve
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi c
                    %mo :
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo →
                    %mi M
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mtext with
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    c: \mathbb{R} \to M \; \text{with} \; c(0) = p
            %p
              As usual, the set of pairs
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo ′
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p, c'(0)), p \in M
              forms a Fréchet manifold, the tangent bundle
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi TM
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TM
          %p The last sentence makes use of the notion of vector bundle, which can be defined exactly as in the finite dimensional setting:
          %h3#vector_bundles Vector bundles
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              %strong vector bundle
            %p
              A Fréchet manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is a Fréchet vector bundle over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              with projection
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi π
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p \in M
              there are charts of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi M
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              such that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is mapped locally to
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ⊂
                    %mi F
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \subset F \times G
              for Fréchet spaces
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ,
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi π
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi
              corresponds to the projection of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ×
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \times G
              to
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p Since, as mentioned before, the dual space of a Fréchet space that is not a Banach space is itself not a Fréchet space, we cannot define the cotangent space canonically as the dual space of the tangent space. Instead we define it directly:
          %h3#differential_forms Differential forms
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              %strong differential form
            %p
              A differential form (a one form)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
              is a smooth map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mi T
                    %mi M
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\alpha: T M \to \mathbb{R}
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi TM
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} TM
              is the tangent bundle.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationBetweenDeffeologicalAndFrechetStructure Relation to diffeological spaces
          %p
            We discuss how Fréchet manifolds form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
            of that of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              Define a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi FrechetManifolds
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi DiffeologicalSpaces
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota \;\colon\; FrechetManifolds \longrightarrow DiffeologicalSpaces
            %p
              from Fréchet manifolds to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
              (and hence to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+spaces"} smooth spaces
              and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+stacks"} smooth stacks
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+manifold"} Fréchet manifold
              for any
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ∈
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \in
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CartSp"} CartSp
              the set of
              = succeed "-plots" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota(X)
              to be the set of smooth functions
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to X
          #FFEmbeddingOfFrechetInDiffeological.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi FrechetManifolds
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi DiffeologicalSpaces
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \colon FrechetManifolds \hookrightarrow DiffeologicalSpaces
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+and+faithful+functor"} full and faithful functor
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Losik94"}> Losik, theorem 3.1.1
            ).
          #CompatibilityWithDiffeologicalMappingSpaces.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi SMoothManifold
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X, Y \in SMoothManifold
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+manifold"} compact manifold
            %p
              Then under this embedding, the diffeological
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
              structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi diff
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)_{diff}
              on the mapping space coincides with the Fréchet manifold structure
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi C
                        %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ,
                      %mi Y
                      %msub
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi Fr
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)_{Fr}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi Fr
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %msub
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi diff
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\iota(C^\infty(X,Y)_{Fr})
                    \\simeq
                    C^\infty(X,Y)_{diff}
                    \\,.
          %p
            This appears as (
            %a{:href => "#Waldorf"}> Waldorf, lemma A.1.7
            ).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %h3#smooth_mapping_spaces Smooth mapping spaces
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X,Y
              two
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+manifolds"} smooth manifolds
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\infty(X,Y)
              is naturally a Fréchet manifold. Under the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              inclusion of Fréchet manifolds into
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+sets"} smooth sets
              (prop.
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FFEmbeddingOfFrechetInDiffeological"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
              formed there.
            %p
              For example
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+loop+space"} smooth loop space
              (i.e. for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = S^1
              the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle"} circle
            %p
              For details on this see at
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold+structure+of+mapping+spaces"} manifold structure of mapping spaces
          %h3#ProjectiveLimitsOfSmoothFiniteDimensionalManifolds Projective limits of smooth finite-dimensional manifolds
          %p
            (see also
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#DodsonGalanisVassiliou15"} Dodson-Galanis-Vassiliou 15
          %p
            Fréchet manifolds may be thought of as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+limits"} projective limits
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+manifolds"} Banach manifolds
            (see the “added remark” at the end of
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "http://math.stackexchange.com/a/53020/58526"} this MO comment
          #RInfinity.num_example
            %h6#example_2 Example
            %p
              The infinite product
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fr%C3%A9chet+space"} Fréchet space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              (
              %a{:href => "Fr&#233;chet+space#ProjRInfinity"}> exmpl.
              ) is of course a Fréchet manifold.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              As a Fréchet manifold,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RInfinity"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+limit"} projective limit
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo ≃
                    %msub
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo ⟵
                      %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^\infty \simeq \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}_n \mathbb{R}^n
            %p formed in the category of Fréchet manifolds.
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The point that needs checking is that for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              any Fréchet manifold, then a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+function"} continuous function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⟶
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \colon X \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^\infty
            %p is smooth as soon as all its components
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi X
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi f
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi n
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f_n \colon X \overset{f}{\longrightarrow} \mathbb{R}^\infty
                    \\overset{p_n}{\longrightarrow} \mathbb{R}
            %p
              are smooth. This is checked for instance in (
              %a{:href => "#Saunders89"}> Saunders 89, lemma 7.1.8
              ).
          %p Conversely:
          #DifferentiableFunctionOutOfRInfinity.num_prop
            %h6#proposition_4 Proposition
            %p A function
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathbb{R}^\infty
                    \\longrightarrow
                    \\mathbb{R}
            %p
              %em out
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              (example
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RInfinity"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+number"} finite number
              of its partial derivative are non-vanishing.
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Saunders89"}> Saunders 89, example 7.1.6
            )
          #JetBundlesAsFrechetManifolds.num_example
            %h6#example_3 Example
            %p
              As a global generalization of the pro-finite dimensional Fréchet manifold
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              of example
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RInfinity"}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundle"} jet bundle
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi E
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %munder
                        %mi lim
                        %mo ⟵
                      %mi k
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mi k
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^\infty E = \underset{\longleftarrow}{\lim}_k J^k E
              is a Fréchet manifold, modeled on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^\infty
              (
              %a{:href => "#Saunders89"}> Saunders 89, chapter 7
              ).
          #DifferenceBetweenProManifoldAndFrecherManifoldStructure.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              Beware, that infinite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundles"} jet bundles
              are also naturally thought of as
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-manifolds"} pro-manifolds
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#JetBundlesAsFrechetManifolds"}
            %p
              A morphism of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-manifolds"} pro-manifolds
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \colon J^\infty E \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p
              is equivalently a function that is “globally of finite order”, in that there exists
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
              and an ordinary smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi f
                      %mi k
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mi k
                    %mi E
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_k \colon J^k E \to \mathbb{R}
              such that
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo =
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi k
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f = f_k \circ p_k
            %p
              But by prop.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DifferentiableFunctionOutOfRInfinity"}
              a morphisms of Fréchet manifolds
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi E
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    f \colon J^\infty E \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}
            %p is only restricted to have finite order of partial derivatives at every point.
            %p
              This is a weaker condition. In fact it seems to be also weaker than the condition of being “locally of finite order” considered in
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "jet+bundle#Takens79"} Takens 79
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              is
              %em locally of finite order
              if for every point in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^\infty E
              there exists a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{N}
              and an open neighbourhood
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_k
              of its image in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mi k
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^k E
              and a smooth function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi f
                      %mi k
                      %mi U
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f^U_k \colon U_k \to \mathbb{R}
              such that restricted to the pre-image of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_k
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi J
                      %mn ∞
                    %mi E
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J^\infty E
              the function
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
              given by
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi k
                      %mo ∘
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f_k \circ p_k
            %p
              Hence it makes sense to speak of
              = succeed "." do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+pro-manifolds"} locally pro-manifolds
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Banach+manifold"} Banach manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+manifold"}> Hilbert manifold
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ILH+manifold"} ILH manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convenient+manifold"} convenient manifold
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/F-star-algebra"} F-star-algebra
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometry+of+physics+--+smooth+sets"} geometry of physics – smooth sets
          %h2#references References
          %p Accounts include:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+S.+Hamilton"}> Richard S. Hamilton
                , Section I.4 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The inverse function theorem of Nash and Moser
                %strong 7
                (1982) 65-222 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1090/S0273-0979-1982-15004-2"}> doi:1982-07-01/S0273-0979-1982-15004-2
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/journals/bulletin-of-the-american-mathematical-society-new-series/volume-7/issue-1/The-inverse-function-theorem-of-Nash-and-Moser/bams/1183549049.full"} euclid:bams/1183549049
            %li#Michor80
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Michor"}> Peter Michor
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Manifolds of differentiable mappings
                %a{:href => "http://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~michor/manifolds_of_differentiable_mappings.pdf"} pdf
            %li#KM
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+Kriegl"}> Andreas Kriegl
                ,
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Michor"} Peter Michor
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/The+convenient+setting+of+global+analysis"} The convenient setting of global analysis
                %strong 53
                AMS (1997) &amp;lbrack;ISBN:978-0-8218-0780-4,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://bookstore.ams.org/surv-53"} ams:surv-53
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.mat.univie.ac.at/~michor/apbookh-ams.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                V. I. Arnold, B. A. Khesin,
                %em Topological methods in hydrodynamics.
                (Springer 1998,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:0902.76001&format=complete"} ZMATH
            %li
              %p
                Boris Khesin, Robert Wendt,
                %em The geometry of infinite-dimensional groups.
                (Springer 2009,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.zentralblatt-math.org/zmath/en/advanced/?q=an:1153.22001&format=complete"} ZMATH
            %li
              %p
                Armen Sergeev, Part I of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Kähler Geometry of Loop Spaces
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/ebooks/mathematical-society-of-japan-memoirs/K%C3%A4hler-Geometry-of-Loop-Spaces/Chapter/Part-I-Preliminary-concepts/10.2969/msjmemoirs/02301C010"} euclid:10.2969/msjmemoirs/02301C010
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia:
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fr%C3%A9chet_manifold"} Fréchet manifold
          %p
            The embedding into
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diffeological+spaces"} diffeological spaces
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Losik92
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Losik"}> Mark Losik
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Fréchet manifolds as diffeologic spaces
              = succeed "." do
                %a{:href => "https://dmitripavlov.org/scans/losik-frechet-manifolds-as-diffeologic-spaces.pdf"} PDF
              = succeed "," do
                %em О многообразиях Фреше как диффеологических пространствах
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://mi.mathnet.ru/eng/ivm4812"} mathnet:ivm4812
          %p and reviewed in
          %ul
            %li#Losik94
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mark+Losik"}> Mark Losik
              , Section 3 of:
              = succeed "." do
                %em Categorical Differential Geometry
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/CTGDC_1994__35_4_274_0"} numdam:CTGDC_1994__35_4_274_0
          %p
            The preservation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+spaces"} mapping spaces
            under this embedding is due to
          %ul
            %li#Waldorf
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Konrad+Waldorf"}> Konrad Waldorf
              ,
              %em Transgression to Loop Spaces and its Inverse I
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0911.3212"}> arXiv:0911.3212
              )
          %p
            Fréchet manifold structure on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/jet+bundles"} jet bundles
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Saunders89
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Saunders"}> David Saunders
                , chapter 7 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The geometry of jet bundles
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 142
            %li#DodsonGalanisVassiliou15
              %p
                C. T. J. Dodson, George Galanis, Efstathios Vassiliou,, p. 109 and section 6.3 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Geometry in a Fréchet Context: A Projective Limit Approach
